1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,190
The Federal Reserve raised interest rates.

2
00:00:02,573 --> 00:00:04,350
And its own interest bill went up.

3
00:00:04,528 --> 00:00:07,450
In September, the Fed raised rates by a quarter point.

4
00:00:07,827 --> 00:00:08,710
Here's the problem:

5
00:00:09,034 --> 00:00:12,470
banks keep roughly three trillion dollars in reserves at the Fed,

6
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:14,030
and the Fed pays them interest.

7
00:00:14,438 --> 00:00:15,970
Just that quarter-point increase

8
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:19,810
would add about seven and a half billion dollars a year in interest costs,

9
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:21,290
if reserves stayed the same.

10
00:00:21,577 --> 00:00:24,610
But much of the Fed's bond income doesn't rise as quickly.

11
00:00:24,801 --> 00:00:26,470
When expenses outweigh income,

12
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:29,650
losses build into what's called a deferred asset.

13
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:34,130
By early October, that balance was roughly two hundred and thirty-four billion dollars.

14
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:36,590
Until future earnings cover it,

15
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:39,150
the Fed's normal payments to the Treasury stay paused.

16
00:00:39,575 --> 00:00:43,570
In twenty twenty-one, those payments were more than one hundred and seven billion dollars.

17
00:00:44,457 --> 00:00:46,130
Higher rates can fight inflation.

18
00:00:46,472 --> 00:00:49,530
But they can also make the Fed's own finances harder to repair.
